::Q1::
Which organ filters blood? {
~Heart
=Kidney
~Liver
~Lung
}

Which route avoids first-pass hepatic metabolism? {
~Oral
=Sublingual
~Topical
~Rectal
}

What does pH 7 indicate? {
=Neutral
~Acidic
~Basic
~Sterile
}

Which dosage form is a liquid oral preparation? {
~Tablet
=Syrup
~Capsule
~Patch
}

Which abbreviation means twice daily? {
~OD
=BID
~TID
~PRN
}

Which device measures body temperature? {
~Stethoscope
=Thermometer
~Sphygmomanometer
~Otoscope
}

A medication is intended to release its active ingredient slowly over twelve hours. Which formulation feature is most important? {
~A brightly colored coating that identifies the medicine
=A controlled-release matrix that regulates drug diffusion
~A sweet flavoring agent that improves patient acceptance
~A larger container that holds additional doses
}

Which statement best describes a suspension? {
~A gas completely dissolved in a liquid
=Insoluble solid particles dispersed in a liquid
~Two liquids mixed uniformly at the molecular level
~A dry powder compressed into a solid unit dose
}

For an enteric-coated tablet, where should drug release primarily occur? {
~In the mouth before swallowing
~In the acidic contents of the stomach
=In the higher-pH environment of the intestine
~Only after the tablet has been excreted unchanged
}

Which factor most directly changes the rate of drug elimination in renal impairment? {
~The patient’s preferred tablet color
=Reduced kidney clearance of the drug and its metabolites
~The number of tablets stored in the dispensing cabinet
~The manufacturer’s logo printed on the carton
}

The following stem has two lines.
Which dosage form is most appropriate when a patient cannot swallow a solid oral medicine? {
~A large unscored tablet intended to be swallowed whole
=An oral liquid formulation that can be measured accurately
~A hard gelatin capsule that must remain intact
~An extended-release tablet that must not be crushed
}

Which term describes an unwanted effect at usual therapeutic doses? {
~Indication
=Adverse effect
~Excipient
~Prescription
}

What is the SI unit for mass? {
~Liter
=Kilogram
~Meter
~Second
}

Which symbol means approximately equal to? {
~≠
=≈
~≤
~≥
}

Which practice best reduces medication administration errors? {
~Relying only on memory during a busy shift
=Confirming the patient, drug, dose, route, and time before administration
~Using an unlabeled container to save preparation time
~Skipping documentation when the patient appears stable
}

Which route commonly uses a patch? {
~Intravenous
=Transdermal
~Inhalational
~Intrathecal
}

Which ingredient is usually pharmacologically inactive? {
~Active moiety
=Excipient
~Antidote
~Receptor
}

Why should a reconstituted antibiotic suspension be shaken before each dose? {
~To make the bottle easier to open
=To redistribute particles and provide a uniform dose
~To convert the suspension into a sterile injectable solution
~To remove the expiration date from the label
}

Which law relates pressure and volume at constant temperature? {
=Boyle’s law
~Charles’s law
~Avogadro’s law
~Dalton’s law
}

Which notation means milligram? {
~mL
=mg
~mcg
~gtt
}
